            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Career Beginnings</strong><br>\nRobert Capa was born in 1913 in Budapest, Hungary, and began his photography career in Berlin before moving to Paris. His early work covered the rise of Hitler, which set the stage for his lifelong dedication to capturing the turmoil of war and conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Spanish Civil War and Iconic Photography</strong><br>\nCapa's coverage of the Spanish Civil War brought him international acclaim, where his photograph \"The Falling Soldier\" became one of the most famous war-time images, raising questions about the role of photography in depicting the reality of conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II and Humanizing Soldiers</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Capa was with the first wave of troops landing on Omaha Beach on D-Day, capturing harrowing images of the invasion. His work is celebrated for its raw depiction of warfare and for humanizing the soldiers in the midst of battle.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
