          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Death and Last Assignment</strong><br>\nCapa died in 1954 after stepping on a landmine while on assignment in Indochina. His tragic death underscored the risks he took to document the front lines and the personal cost of war journalism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Photojournalism</strong><br>\nCapa's legacy in photojournalism is profound; he set the standard for what it means to be a war photographer. His action-packed, close-up shots of conflict zones have influenced generations of photographers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Remembering Capa's Work</strong><br>\nRobert Capa's photographs go beyond the battles they capture; they serve as historical documentation of the 20th century's conflicts and their human toll. His work remains a seminal reference for those studying war and its impact.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
